        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Abolitionism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">Arose among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> and evangelical Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>; spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, and drew strength from the resistance of the enslaved</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1787–1888: from the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> committee against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>’s Golden Law; Quaker protests reach back to 1688, and campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and Asia went on into the 20th century</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-clarkson">Thomas Clarkson</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaudah-equiano">Olaudah Equiano</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joaquim-nabuco">Joaquim Nabuco</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> was outlawed and suppressed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> abolished throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> by 1888; owners were often compensated, while the freed rarely received land or equal rights</span></div>
