    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Abolitionism grew from Quaker and evangelical protest into the first great campaigns of petitions, boycotts and public testimony, aimed first at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> and then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> itself.</li><li>Enslaved people fought for their own freedom throughout: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>, great revolts in the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and countless escapes pushed governments towards abolition.</li><li>By the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lei-aurea">Golden Law</a> in 1888 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> had been abolished throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, but freedom rarely brought the formerly enslaved land, compensation or equality.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lei-aurea">Golden Law</a> of 13 May 1888, which ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the last country of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to keep it, has just two articles: the first declares slavery extinct in Brazil, the second revokes all provisions to the contrary.</p></aside>
    </section>

