    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1787</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> and Anglicans found the Society for Effecting the Abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1791</span><span class="rf-text">Enslaved people rise in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>; their revolution ends <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> there and founds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in 1804</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1807</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> ban the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1831</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-sharpe">Samuel Sharpe</a>’s rising begins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison">William Lloyd Garrison</a> founds The Liberator</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1833</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> Abolition Act frees the enslaved across most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> from 1834</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in their colonies</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1865</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a> abolishes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1888</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabel-princess-imperial-of-brazil">Princess Isabel</a> signs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lei-aurea">Golden Law</a>, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the last country of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to keep it</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zong-massacre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zong-massacre.jpg" alt="Zong massacre" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zong-massacre">Zong massacre</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slave-ship.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">J. M. W. Turner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was legal throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and accepted by most churches, governments and legal codes. Between the 16th and the 19th centuries European and American ships carried some 12.5 million Africans across the Atlantic, of whom about 10.7 million survived the voyage known as the Middle Passage. Most were bought on the African coast from African traders and states and taken to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>; others went to the mines and cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> and the tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> fields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The trade reached its height in the 18th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> were its leading carriers.</p><p>Resistance began with slavery itself. Captives rose on board ship, ran away and formed maroon communities, such as Palmares in north-eastern Brazil, which held out for most of the 17th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaican-maroons">Jamaican Maroons</a>, who won treaties recognising their freedom from the British in 1739–1740; Tacky’s Revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in 1760 took months to suppress. Among free people the first sustained opposition came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a>. In 1688 residents of Germantown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, issued the first organised protest against slavery in the English colonies, and in the 18th century John Woolman and Anthony Benezet persuaded the Society of Friends to expel members who traded in slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> attacked slavery as unjust or wasteful, and the evangelical leader John Wesley condemned it in his Thoughts upon Slavery of 1774.</p><p>The 1770s and 1780s brought a crisis of conscience. In 1772, in a case brought with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granville-sharp">Granville Sharp</a>, Lord Mansfield ruled that James Somerset, an enslaved man in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, could not be forcibly removed to be sold in Jamaica, a decision widely, if wrongly, believed to have ended slavery in England. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>’s appeal to natural rights exposed the contradiction of slaveholding republicans: Vermont’s constitution of 1777 banned adult slavery, Pennsylvania passed a gradual abolition act in 1780 and Massachusetts courts ended slavery in the early 1780s. In 1781 the crew of the slave ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zong-massacre">Zong</a> threw more than 130 Africans overboard, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> owners then claimed insurance for them as lost cargo; when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaudah-equiano">Olaudah Equiano</a> brought the case to Sharp in 1783, it became a scandal.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/olaudah-equiano"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olaudah-equiano.jpg" alt="Olaudah Equiano" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/olaudah-equiano">Olaudah Equiano</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olaudah_Equiano_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_15399.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The British campaigners chose to attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> first. Ending the trade seemed more achievable than freeing the enslaved, touched no one’s legal property in people and was expected to force planters to treat the enslaved better, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> itself would wither. To win public opinion they used new methods: hundreds of petitions to Parliament, evidence gathered from sailors and ships’ surgeons, a print of the slave ship Brookes showing captives packed below deck, a boycott of slave-grown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> in 1791–1792, and an emblem produced by the potter Josiah Wedgwood: a kneeling African in chains asking “Am I not a man and a brother?”</p><p>Christians argued that Africans were children of the same God and that slavery was a sin; others appealed to natural rights and the words of the American Declaration of Independence, “all men are created equal”, or to the economists’ claim that free labour was more productive. Over time the goal widened from the trade to slavery itself and hardened from gradual to immediate emancipation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s Anti-Slavery Society, founded in 1823, at first sought “mitigation and gradual abolition”; in 1824 Elizabeth Heyrick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a> demanded Immediate, Not Gradual Abolition, and the movement soon came round to her view. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison">William Lloyd Garrison</a> declared in the first issue of The Liberator in 1831: “I will not equivocate — I will not excuse — I will not retreat a single inch — and I will be heard.” Some white reformers backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, founded in 1816 to settle free Black Americans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, but most Black abolitionists rejected it.</p><p>The enslaved had aims of their own, often more far-reaching: freedom for themselves and their families, land, wages, an end to the whip and the right to worship as they chose. They pursued them by flight, by buying their freedom, by lawsuits and by revolt. Formerly enslaved writers made the case in their own words: Ottobah Cugoano in 1787, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaudah-equiano">Olaudah Equiano</a> in his Interesting Narrative of 1789, Mary Prince, whose History of 1831 was the first account of the life of an enslaved Black woman published in Britain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a> in his Narrative of 1845. Women formed their own anti-slavery societies, and the cause led many of them, such as the American sisters Sarah and Angelina Grimké, into the struggle for women’s rights.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/baptist-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baptist-war.jpg" alt="Baptist War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/baptist-war">Baptist War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duperly_(1833)_Destruction_of_the_Roehampton_Estate_January_1832.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adolphe Duperly, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Society for Effecting the Abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade</a> was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 22 May 1787 by twelve men, nine of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a>, Member of Parliament for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>, carried the cause into the House of Commons, which voted down his motion in 1791. In August that year the enslaved of the Northern Plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>’s richest colony, rose in revolt. French commissioners freed them in 1793, and on 4 February 1794 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in all French colonies. In 1802 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sent an army to retake the colony, and French forces restored slavery in Guadeloupe, but Saint-Domingue’s Black and mixed-race generals defeated the expedition, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> proclaimed the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> on 1 January 1804. In March 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> banned its slave trade, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> banned the import of slaves from 1 January 1808.</p><p>In the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> the enslaved rose in Barbados in 1816 and Demerara in 1823, and at Christmas 1831 tens of thousands in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> followed the Baptist deacon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-sharpe">Samuel Sharpe</a> in a strike that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptist-war">Baptist War</a>. Its brutal suppression, reported by missionaries, helped persuade a newly reformed Parliament to pass the Slavery Abolition Act of 1833. On 1 August 1834 some 800,000 people ceased to be slaves, though most had to work unpaid for much of each week as “apprentices” until protests ended the system in 1838. In 1848 France, by a decree drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-schoelcher">Victor Schoelcher</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, after a rising on St Croix, freed the enslaved in their colonies.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> made slavery ever more profitable, Nat Turner’s revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> in 1831 was crushed and followed by harsher slave laws. The American Anti-Slavery Society was founded in 1833, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a> helped fugitives reach the free states and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. The Fugitive Slave Act of 1850, Harriet Beecher Stowe’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uncle-toms-cabin">Uncle Tom’s Cabin</a> of 1852 and John Brown’s raid on the federal armoury at Harpers Ferry in 1859 deepened the crisis. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a> of 1 January 1863 declared free the enslaved in the rebel states, some 180,000 Black men served in the Union army, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a>, ratified in December 1865, ended slavery throughout the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/law-of-the-free-womb">Law of the Free Womb</a> of 1871 had declared free the children born to enslaved mothers, came last: as abolitionist clubs spread and the enslaved fled the plantations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> in their thousands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabel-princess-imperial-of-brazil">Princess Isabel</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lei-aurea">Golden Law</a> on 13 May 1888.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman.jpg" alt="Harriet Tubman" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman">Harriet Tubman</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harriet_Tubman_(circa_1885).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horatio Seymour Squyer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granville-sharp">Granville Sharp</a>, a government clerk and scholar, took up the legal cases of enslaved Africans; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-clarkson">Thomas Clarkson</a> rode thousands of miles to gather evidence in the slaving ports; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a>, an evangelical Christian, led the cause in Parliament for more than thirty years before Thomas Fowell Buxton took it over in the 1820s. The poet Hannah More wrote verse for it. Black Londoners spoke for themselves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaudah-equiano">Olaudah Equiano</a> wrote that he was born in what is now south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and enslaved as a child; the historian Vincent Carretta, citing records that give his birthplace as Carolina, has questioned this, though many scholars accept his account. He bought his freedom in 1766, and his book went through nine editions in his lifetime. With Ottobah Cugoano he belonged to the Sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, a group of Black campaigners in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><p>Leaders of the enslaved shaped events as decisively. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a>, a formerly enslaved coachman, rose to command the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> and to govern the colony, until the French seized him in 1802; he died in 1803 in the prison of Fort de Joux in the Jura mountains, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> led the last war for independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-sharpe">Samuel Sharpe</a>, who had planned a peaceful strike, was hanged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montego-bay">Montego Bay</a> in May 1832. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the priest Henri Grégoire and the journalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-schoelcher">Victor Schoelcher</a> campaigned for the rights of Black people in the colonies. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a>, who escaped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> in 1838, became one of the movement’s most famous orators and founded his own newspaper; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman">Harriet Tubman</a>, who escaped in 1849, went back some thirteen times to lead about seventy people to freedom; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sojourner-truth">Sojourner Truth</a> preached against slavery across the northern states. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> the deputy and later diplomat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joaquim-nabuco">Joaquim Nabuco</a> set out the case in O Abolicionismo in 1883; other leaders were the engineer André Rebouças, the journalist José do Patrocínio and Luís Gama, born free but sold into slavery by his father at the age of ten, who as a self-taught lawyer won freedom in the courts for more than 500 people, by his own count.</p><p>Behind them stood a mass following. Hundreds of thousands of people signed petitions, many of them women, who also organised the boycotts of slave-grown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and founded their own anti-slavery associations. Black churches and vigilance committees in the cities of the northern United States sheltered fugitives, and the enslaved themselves passed news of escape routes, of debates in distant parliaments and of risings elsewhere from plantation to plantation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/west-africa-squadron"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-africa-squadron.jpg" alt="West Africa Squadron" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/west-africa-squadron">West Africa Squadron</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HMS_Black_Joke_(1827)_and_prizes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Irwin John David Bevan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 1888 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was illegal everywhere in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. The new republics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> had begun to free their slaves during and after their wars of independence, Chile in 1823 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1829 among the first; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> followed in 1863, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> in 1873 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 1886. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, condemned by the powers at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815, was hunted at sea by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa-squadron">West Africa Squadron</a>, which captured about 1,600 slave ships and freed some 150,000 Africans; the traffic ended only when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> closed its ports to it in 1850 and the last voyages to Cuba ceased in the 1860s. Abolition was among the first great causes in which ordinary men and women organised across borders on behalf of people they had never met, and the treaties against the trade were among the first international agreements made on humanitarian grounds.</p><p>Freedom came on terms set by the former masters. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> paid £20 million to slave owners and nothing to the enslaved; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> also compensated owners in 1849, and in 1825 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> was forced, under the threat of French warships, to promise France 150 million francs for recognition of its independence, a burden that weighed on it for generations. Apprenticeship, vagrancy laws and low wages tied many of the freed to the plantations, and planters brought in indentured labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to replace them. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the promise of land made to some freed families in 1865 was revoked by President Andrew Johnson, and after the end of Reconstruction in 1877 the southern states imposed segregation and stripped most Black citizens of the vote. In Brazil abolition came without land or schooling for the freed, and the anger of former slaveholders helped bring down the monarchy in 1889.</p><p>Slavery also continued beyond the Atlantic world, in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. European powers cited the suppression of the slave trade to justify conquests in Africa, as at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> Anti-Slavery Conference of 1889–1890, while imposing forced labour of their own. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> adopted a Slavery Convention in 1926, and the last country to abolish slavery in law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, did so only in 1981.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/eric-williams"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eric-williams.jpg" alt="Eric Williams" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/eric-williams">Eric Williams</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eric_Williams_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joop van Bilsen for Anefo, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Abolitionism was the model for many later campaigns. Mass petitions, consumer boycotts, emblems, investigative reports, public meetings and the testimony of victims were taken up by the campaigners for women’s suffrage, by those who exposed forced labour in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> and by the human rights movements of the 20th century. The British and Foreign Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> Society, founded in 1839, survives as Anti-Slavery International, often described as the oldest international human rights organisation.</p><p>Historians have long argued about why abolition happened. Early 20th-century historians told a story of moral triumph led by Wilberforce and his evangelical allies. In Capitalism and Slavery of 1944 the Trinidadian historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-williams">Eric Williams</a>, later his country’s first prime minister, argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> abolished the trade and slavery when the West Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> economy was declining and no longer served British capitalism. Seymour Drescher replied in Econocide of 1977 that the British slave economy was still expanding when the trade was banned, so that abolition was driven by popular mobilisation at an economic cost. David Brion Davis traced the changes in religious and moral thought that made slavery seem a problem at all. C. L. R. James’s The Black Jacobins of 1938 put the rebels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> at the centre of the story, and later historians such as Robin Blackburn and Manisha Sinha have stressed the part played by the enslaved and by Black abolitionists.</p><p>The end of slavery is now widely commemorated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>’s International Day for the Remembrance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade</a> and its Abolition, on 23 August, recalls the night in 1791 when the rising in Saint-Domingue began. Keti Koti, on 1 July, marks the abolition of 1863 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, and Juneteenth, on 19 June, recalls the announcement of freedom in Galveston, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, in 1865 and became a federal holiday in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 2021. Databases of the slave voyages and of the British compensation claims have brought to light both the scale of the trade and the people who profited from it.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Abolitionism was the movement that between 1787 and 1888 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Quaker and evangelical campaigners, Black writers and the enslaved themselves, through petitions, boycotts, escapes and revolts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>, forced governments to act, though freedom rarely brought the formerly enslaved land, compensation or equal rights.</em></p>

